			"description": "Excessive Data Exposure\r\n\r\nA typical web frontend for this endpoint would render a comment's text, author, and map position, and quietly ignore any other fields the API happens to send back. The bug is in the response itself, not in any particular client: inspect the raw JSON (via curl, or a browser's Network tab if a frontend were calling this) and you'll see a field the UI was never meant to show.\r\n\r\n### curl: read the raw API response\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -s https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment | python3 -m json.tool\r\n```\r\nLook at every key in the object, not just the ones a UI would display -- one of them is backend-only data (`deviceid`) that leaked into the public response.\r\n\r\n### curl: the rest of the flow\r\n```bash\r\n# register\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/user \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"username\":\"u1\",\"name\":\"U\",\"password\":\"p1\"}'\r\n\r\n# log in (returns only your own username on success -- this endpoint itself isn't the leak)\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/user/login \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"username\":\"u1\",\"password\":\"p1\"}'\r\n\r\n# post a comment, then re-run the GET above -- yours shows up with the same extra field\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api3/comment \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"postid\":\"1\",\"deviceid\":\"whatever-a-client-would-send\",\"latitude\":\"0\",\"longitude\":\"0\",\"commenttext\":\"hi\",\"username\":\"u1\"}'\r\n```",
			"event": [
